#DESCRIPTION
##  Calculus: Three-dimensional space
##ENDDESCRIPTION


## DBsubject(Geometry)
## DBchapter(Vector geometry)
## DBsection(Coordinate systems)
## Date(11/29/2011)
## Institution(University of Minnesota)
## Author(Justin Sukiennik)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus: Concepts and Contexts')
## AuthorText1('Stewart')
## EditionText1('5')
## Section1('12.1')
## Problem1('10')
## KEYWORDS('calculus', 'three-dimensional', 'multi-variable', 'points', 'distance formula')

#####################################################################

DOCUMENT();        # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "PGML.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl"
);

#####################################################################

Context("Numeric");

$a = random(-9,-1,1);
$b = random(2,9,1);
$c = random(-14,-10,1);

$ans1 = Compute("abs($c)");
$ans2 = Compute("abs($a)");
$ans3 = Compute("abs($b)");
$ans4 = Compute("sqrt(($b)^2+($c)^2)");
$ans5 = Compute("sqrt(($a)^2+($c)^2)");
$ans6 = Compute("sqrt(($a)^2+($b)^2)");

#####################################################################

BEGIN_PGML
Find the distance from [`([$a],[$b],[$c])`] to each of the following:

a. The [`xy`]-plane.  
   [_]{$ans1}{20}
a. The [`yz`]-plane.  
   [_]{$ans2}{20}
a. The [`xz`]-plane.  
   [_]{$ans3}{20}
a. The [`x`]-axis.  
   [_]{$ans4}{20}
a. The [`y`]-axis.  
   [_]{$ans5}{20}
a. The [`z`]-axis.  
   [_]{$ans6}{20}

END_PGML

$bc=$b*$b+$c*$c;
$ac=$a*$a+$c*$c;
$ab=$a*$a+$b*$b;
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

a. The distance from a point to the [`xy`]-plane is the absolute value of 
the [`z`]-coordinate of the point. Thus, the distance is
 [` \left| [$c]  \right| = [$ans1]`].
a. The distance from a point to the [`yz`]-plane is the absolute value of 
the [`x`]-coordinate of the point. Thus, the distance is
 [` \left| [$a]  \right| = [$ans2]`].
a. The distance from a point to the [`xz`]-plane is the absolute value of 
the [`y`]-coordinate of the point. Thus, the distance is
 [` \left| [$b]  \right| = [$ans3]`].
a. The point on the [`x`]-axis closest to [`([$a], [$b], [$c])`] is the point
[` ([$a], 0,0 )`], (Approach the [`x`]-axis perpendicularly.)  
The distance  from [`([$a], [$b], [$c])`] to the [`x`]-axis is the distance between these two points:  
[` \sqrt{ ([$a]-[$a])^2 +([$b]-0)^2 +([$c]-0)^2} = \sqrt{([$b])^2+([$c])^2} = 
\sqrt{[$bc]}`]
a. The point on the [`y`]-axis closest to [`([$a], [$b], [$c])`] is the point
[` (0, [$b],0 )`], (Approach the [`y`]-axis perpendicularly.)  
The distance  from [`([$a], [$b], [$c])`] to the [`y`]-axis is the distance between these two points:  
[` \sqrt{ ([$a]-0)^2 +([$b]-[$b])^2 +([$c]-0)^2} = \sqrt{([$a])^2+([$c])^2} = 
\sqrt{[$ac]}`]
a. The point on the [`z`]-axis closest to [`([$a], [$b], [$c])`] is the point
[` (0, 0,[$c] )`], (Approach the [`z`]-axis perpendicularly.)  
The distance  from [`([$a], [$b], [$c])`] to the [`z`]-axis is the distance between these two points:  
[` \sqrt{ ([$a]-0)^2 +([$b]-0)^2 +([$c]-[$c])^2} = \sqrt{([$a])^2+([$b])^2} = 
\sqrt{[$ab]}`]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();      # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
